{"status": "done", "model": "gpt-6-astra", "effort": "high", "n_calls": 22, "api_errors": 4, "started": 1790558545.803169, "usage": {"input": 94863, "output": 2119, "reasoning": 575, "cached": 80896}, "items": [{"role": "developer", "content": "You are an autonomous software/ML engineering agent working in a Linux sandbox (Python 3.10 + numpy; no internet package installs). There is no human available: never ask questions, make reasonable decisions and keep working until the task is fully complete. Use the `shell` tool to inspect files, run experiments and edit code. When you are completely done, reply with a brief final summary and do not call any tool in that final message."}, {"role": "user", "content": "# Postmortem: the fine-tune that looked fine offline\n\nRelease `ft-2026.09` shipped last week and online quality on the **watched slice** dropped. The offline\neval suite did not catch it - that is why it shipped.\n\nWhat the release report says:\n\n| | before (`ft-2026.08`) | after (`ft-2026.09`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| offline, watched slice | 0.8687 | 0.8740 |\n| offline, everything else | ~0.79 | 0.7906 |\n| online, watched slice | 0.8667 | **0.7417** |\n\nFour knobs describe a fine-tune in this project. This release changed **three** of them, each for its own\nreason, and none of those reasons has gone away. Here is the recipe, what shipped, and how far back each\none may be moved:\n\n    knob       recipe   shipped   may be restored only as far as\n    capacity   1.0      0.641     0.759     student capacity headroom on the watched slice\n    mixture    1.0      0.642     0.760     the watched slice's share of the fine-tuning mixture\n    lr_scale   1.0      1.295     1.198     fine-tuning learning rate vs the recipe default\n    leak       0.0      ?         -        fraction of the eval slice that overlaps the training set\n\n**One of those three changes caused the online regression. The release report cannot tell you which** -\nall three shipped together, and the offline number came out clean. `leak` is not a knob anyone sets: it is\na property of the eval slice, it is *measured*, this quarter's overlap audit is still open, and the `0.0`\nabove is the recipe's assumption rather than a measurement of what shipped.\n\n`capacity` and `mixture` cannot go above their recipe values - there is no more serving budget and no more\nlicensed data.\n\n## The lab\n\nRun `/app/bin/lab spec` first. Budget: **20 re-runs**.\n\n* `lab rerun` re-runs the fine-tune with knobs you choose and gives you the **offline** numbers (1 re-run,\n  more if it has to retrain);\n* `lab canary` serves a config to live traffic and gives you the **online** number - this is the number that\n  regressed, and it costs **4 re-runs** a go;\n* `lab decontaminate` re-scores a config on an eval slice with the train-overlapping items removed (1 re-run).\n\nKnobs you leave out of a config keep their **release** value, not their recipe value.\n\n## Deliverable: `/app/report.json`\n\n    {\"cause\": \"<one of ['capacity', 'mixture', 'lr_scale']>\",\n     \"fix\":   {\"<knob>\": <value>, ...}}\n\n## How it is graded (mechanically, against the simulator; only the file counts)\n\n1. **R0** - `report.json` parses, names a cause from ['capacity', 'mixture', 'lr_scale'] and gives a fix.\n2. **R1** - running your fix recovers **>= 90%** of what is reachable online under the release\n   constraint, measured on 3 hidden re-runs.\n3. **R2** - your declared cause survives the grader's counterfactual: resetting *only* that knob must\n   recover the regression, and holding *only* that knob at its release value must reproduce it.\n4. **R3** - your fix actually moves the knob you blamed.\n\nAll four must hold. Note what R1 and R3 do together: naming the right knob is not a fix, and a fix that\nrecovers quality by accident does not tell you the cause.\n\nWrite `/app/report.json` and stop.\n"}, {"id": "fc_0fe87e2dae0e0849016ab9c15ce94487d2b096059d01baca81", "type": "function_call", "arguments": "{\"command\":[\"/app/bin/lab\",\"spec\"],\"timeout_ms\":150000,\"workdir\":\"/app\"}", "call_id": "call_KFZEQkf8T71bOWpRF9pCmn5I", "name": "shell"}, {"type": "function_call_output", "call_id": "call_KFZEQkf8T71bOWpRF9pCmn5I", "output": "{\"exit_code\": 0, \"duration_s\": 0.0, \"output\": \"{\\n \\\"ops\\\": {\\n  \\\"rerun\\\": {\\n   \\\"args\\\": {\\n    \\\"config\\\": \\\"object with any of ['capacity', 'leak', 'mixture', 'lr_scale']; omitted knobs keep their RELEASE value\\\",\\n    \\\"reps\\\": \\\"independent repeats, 1..3 (default 1)\\\"\\n   },\\n   \\\"cost\\\": \\\"1 re-run per rep, x1.5-2 when capacity or mixture is changed (retraining)\\\",\\n   \\\"returns\\\": \\\"per repeat: offline_slice, offline_other; plus their means\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"canary\\\": {\\n   \\\"args\\\": {\\n    \\\"config\\\": \\\"object, same shape as rerun\\\",\\n    \\\"reps\\\": \\\"1..3 (default 1)\\\"\\n   },\\n   \\\"cost\\\": \\\"4 re-runs per rep, x1.5-2 when capacity or mixture is changed\\\",\\n   \\\"returns\\\": \\\"the ONLINE slice number of that config, averaged over the repeats\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"decontaminate\\\": {\\n   \\\"args\\\": {\\n    \\\"config\\\": \\\"object, same shape as rerun\\\"\\n   },\\n   \\\"cost\\\": \\\"1 re-run\\\",\\n   \\\"returns\\\": \\\"the offline slice number of that config on a decontaminated eval slice\\\"\\n  }\\n },\\n \\\"deliverable\\\": \\\"/app/report.json (see /app/docs/report_format.md)\\\",\\n \\\"budget_total\\\": 20.0,\\n \\\"budget_left\\\": 20.0\\n}\\n\"}"}, {"id": "fc_0fe87e2dae0e0849016ab9c160a91087d2809cd75f415f7b9b", "type": "function_call", "arguments": "{\"command\":[\"bash\",\"-lc\",\"ls -la /app && cat /app/docs/report_format.md && /app/bin/lab --help\"],\"timeout_ms\":150000,\"workdir\":\"/app\"}", "call_id": "call_G7lzYIkj2Lc27aquCKRRfsbM", "name": "shell"}, {"type": "function_call_output", "call_id": "call_G7lzYIkj2Lc27aquCKRRfsbM", "output": "{\"exit_code\": 0, \"duration_s\": 0.0, \"output\": \"total 16\\ndrwx------  4 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 4096 Sep 28 01:22 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 10 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat  260 Sep 28 01:22 ..\\n-rw-r--r--  1 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat   32 Sep 28 01:22 .lab_token\\ndrwx------  2 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 4096 Sep 28 01:22 bin\\ndrwx------  2 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 4096 Sep 28 01:22 docs\\n# report.json\\n\\n    {\\n      \\\"cause\\\": \\\"<one of ['capacity', 'mixture', 'lr_scale']>\\\",\\n      \\\"fix\\\": {\\\"<knob>\\\": 0.0,          // the knob you blamed, moved as far as the release note allows\\n              \\\"<other knob>\\\": 0.0}    // and whatever else you had to re-tune around it\\n    }\\n\\nThe values above are placeholders, not a worked example - fill in what you measured.\\n\\nKnobs you leave out keep their **release** value. The grader clamps `fix` to the same release constraint the\\nlab enforces, so asking for a value beyond the cap silently gets you the cap.\\n`lab` - client for the simulated lab.\\n\\n  lab spec                      what the lab offers, costs, your remaining budget\\n  lab status                    budget spent / left\\n  lab history [last=20]         your recent calls and their results\\n  lab <op> key=value ...        run an operation (see `lab spec`)\\n  lab <op> --json '{...}'       same, arguments as one JSON object (use for lists / nested values)\\n  lab <op> --file args.json     same, arguments read from a JSON file\\n\\nEvery successful call is also appended to /app/lab_log.jsonl.  Results are JSON on stdout.\\n\"}"}, {"id": "fc_0fe87e2dae0e0849016ab9c164787c87d28a7cc656061f889b", "type": "function_call", "arguments": "{\"command\":[\"/app/bin/lab\",\"decontaminate\",\"--json\",\"{\\\"config\\\":{}}\"],\"timeout_ms\":150000,\"workdir\":\"/app\"}", "call_id": "call_fVP8MTTJQAZO3x2sMSIxNwgt", "name": "shell"}, {"type": "function_call_output", "call_id": "call_fVP8MTTJQAZO3